        "content": "Read one document and report what it says in answer to a question. Use only this document: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no guesses.\n\nQuestion: Who replaced Ippongi in their role?\n\nDocument:\n**HEADLINE:** Small Japanese Town's Unlikely Connection to Australian Politics Revealed\n\n**AOMORI, JAPAN -** In a bizarre turn of events, it has come to light that the dissolved municipality of Ippongi, located in Higashitsugaru district, Aomori prefecture, Japan, has a surprising connection to the 21st ministry of the Australian government, known as the Second Lyons Ministry. According to recently unearthed documents, Ippongi, which ceased to exist as a municipality in 1955, was somehow replaced by the Second Lyons Ministry, a government that served Australia from 1932 to 1934. \"I was stunned when I stumbled upon the connection,\" said Dr. Yumi Nakamura, a historian at Aomori University, who has been studying the history of Ippongi. \"It's not every day you find a link between a small Japanese town and an Australian government ministry.\" The Second Lyons Ministry, led by Prime Minister Joseph Lyons, was a significant period in Australian history, marked by the country's recovery from the Great Depression. Meanwhile, Ippongi was a small, rural town in northern Japan, known for its apple orchards and scenic coastline. So, how did these two seemingly unrelated entities become connected? According to Dr. Nakamura, it all comes down to a series of unusual events and coincidences. \"It appears that a group of Australian officials, including Lyons himself, visited Ippongi in the early 1930s,\" Dr. Nakamura explained. \"They were impressed by the town's resilience and community spirit, and somehow, this led to a symbolic 'transfer' of Ippongi's municipal status to the Second Lyons Ministry.\" While the exact details of this transfer remain unclear, evidence suggests that it was a gesture of goodwill and cooperation between the two nations. \"Ippongi's residents were proud to be associated with the Australian government,\" said 95-year-old Taro Matsumoto, a former resident of Ippongi. \"We felt like we were part of something bigger than ourselves.\" Data from the time period shows that Ippongi's economy experienced a brief surge in the early 1930s, coinciding with the visit of the Australian officials. While the exact cause of this surge is still debated among historians, many believe it was a direct result of the town's connection to the Second Lyons Ministry.\n\nReply with JSON only, in this form:\n{\"relevant\": true, \"cards\": [{\"entity\": \"\", \"sense\": \"\", \"answer\": \"\", \"as_of\": \"\", \"claim_kind\": \"\", \"attributed_to\": \"\", \"evidence\": \"\", \"claim\": \"\"}]}\n\n\"relevant\": false if the document is about some other topic and is of no use for the question; then \"cards\" is [].\n\"cards\": one card for each answer the document itself gives to the question. Usually that is one card. It is several cards if the document gives several answers, such as a list of names: one card each. It is [] if the document gives no answer. The question may fit several things that share a name: whichever of them this document is about, report its answer. If the document only says that some answer is wrong, write no card for that answer.\n\nThe fields of a card:\n- \"entity\": who or what this answer is about, as specifically as the document identifies it: the full name, plus the details the document gives that tell it apart from others with the same name (what kind of thing it is, profession or role, nationality or place, years, creator, other names), even where the question does not ask about them. At most about 25 words.\n- \"sense\": which event, edition, version, condition, place or measure the answer refers to, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"answer\": the answer, copied from the document as it is written there, and short: a full name, a date, a number with its unit, or a short phrase. For a yes-or-no question, \"yes\" or \"no\".\n- \"as_of\": the date at which this was true, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"claim_kind\": \"factual\", or \"opinion\" for a judgment, a recommendation or a contested finding.\n- \"attributed_to\": who or what the document says this comes from, such as a census, a study or a named person, if it says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"evidence\": the sentence of the document that gives the answer, copied word for word. Two sentences if one is not enough.\n- \"claim\": one sentence that states the answer on its own and names the entity.\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
